 "intro": "The influenza pandemic of 1918–1920, caused by an H1N1 influenza A virus and misleadingly called the Spanish flu, infected perhaps a third of the world's people and killed somewhere between 17 and 50 million, possibly more. It came in waves: a mild one in the spring of 1918, a devastating one from late August to December that by most accounts struck young adults unusually hard, and a third through 1919. The last year of the First World War spread it: troopships, training camps and crowded hospitals carried it from port to port and then along rivers and railways inland. This map follows the three waves, shading each region by how hard it was hit and marking the cities where the sources record its arrival or its dead.",
 "outcome": "Between 17 and 50 million died, possibly 100 million; smaller waves followed in 1920 before the virus became a seasonal flu.",
 "uncertainty": "Where the pandemic began is unknown. The first confirmed cases were in the United States: Haskell County, Kansas in early 1918 (Barry), though a 2018 study found the Kansas cases milder than those in New York and leaned only towards a North American origin. Others propose the British army camp at Étaples in France, hit by a similar illness in late 1916; Austria in early 1917; or northern China, where a respiratory illness in November 1917 preceded the dispatch of the Chinese Labour Corps to France. The alternative sites are marked. Regional shading is schematic and the intensities are relative, read from the dates and tolls the sources give; China's toll in particular is poorly known. The ships' tracks are inferred from the ports they linked.",
