   "id": "kansas",
   "title": "Kansas, early 1918",
   "time": "January–March 1918",
   "narrative": "The first cases that can be pinned down came from rural Haskell County in Kansas, where a local doctor reported a severe influenza in January and February 1918. On 4 March an army cook at Camp Funston, a huge training camp at Fort Riley some 320 km away, reported sick, and within days 522 men at the camp had followed. By 11 March the virus was in Queens, New York. The United States was at war, and its camps were filling with recruits bound for France.",
