  {
   "id": "across-the-atlantic",
   "title": "Across the Atlantic",
   "time": "April–May 1918",
   "narrative": "Troops of the American Expeditionary Forces carried the disease from the training camps to the French ports, and by mid-April it was on the Western Front. It ran through France, Britain, Italy and Spain and in May reached Wrocław and Odessa, North Africa, India and Japan. This first wave was mild: in Madrid fewer than a thousand people died of it in May and June. But neutral Spain's uncensored newspapers reported it freely while the warring countries kept quiet, and so it became known as the Spanish flu.",
