  {
   "id": "second-wave",
   "title": "Brest, Boston and Freetown",
   "time": "August–September 1918",
   "narrative": "In the second half of August a far deadlier wave began. It probably reached Boston and Freetown in Sierra Leone on ships from Brest, where American troops or French naval recruits had likely brought it. From the Boston Navy Yard and Camp Devens it moved to other army camps and onto troopships bound for Europe; from Freetown it spread along the West African coast, rivers and railways. In September ships bringing men of the South African Native Labour Corps home from France landed it in South Africa. The Allied force in North Russia brought it to Arkhangelsk, and from the east it entered Iran at Mashhad.",
