  {
   "id": "armistice",
   "title": "Armistice and the Pacific",
   "time": "November–December 1918",
   "narrative": "Crowds celebrating the armistice of 11 November set off fresh outbreaks in places such as Lima and Nairobi, and the disease reached Ethiopia. Ships from New Zealand, which was slow to stop infected vessels leaving its ports, carried it to the Pacific islands: Tonga lost 8% of its people and Fiji 5%, about 9,000. In Western Samoa, under New Zealand occupation, nine in ten people were infected and three in ten adult men died. Iran, already in famine, lost between 8% and 22% of its population. By December the second wave was largely over.",
